    <DataNode elementId="Beta-Lactams" textLabel="a β-lactam" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="35627" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="540" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">This compound class stands for |FRAME: Lactams lactams| in which the amide bond is contained within a four-membered ring, which includes the amide nitrogen and the carbonyl carbon. &amp;beta;-Lactam compounds imitate the naturally occurring |FRAME: D-ALA-D-ALA| substrate for the group of enzymes known as penicillin binding proteins (PBPs), which cross-link the peptidoglycan in bacterial cell wall, and thus act as powerful antibiotics |CITS: [6109324]|. Naturally occurring |FRAME: Beta-Lactams &quot;&amp;beta;-lactam antibiotics&quot;| are classified into several groups according to their chemical structure: In |FRAME: Monolactams monolactams| the &amp;beta;-lactam ring is not fused to another ring. This class includes compounds such as the |FRAME: Nocardicins nocardicins| |CITS: [3427723]| and the |FRAME: Monobactams monobactams| |CITS: [3909315]|. In |FRAME: Penams penams| the &amp;beta;-lactam ring is fused to a saturated five atom ring (a 4:5 structure). Penams contain a sulfur atom at position 1 of the five atom ring (for example, the |FRAME: PENICILLIN penicillins|) |CITS: [6335599]|. Related classes include the |FRAME: Carbapenams carbapenams|, which contain a carbon atom in that position |CITS: [12890666]|, and the |FRAME: Clavams oxopenams| (also known as clavams), which contain an oxygen atom at that position (e.g. |FRAME: CPD-9261|) |CITS: [3304182]|. |FRAME: Penems Penems| are molecues similar to penams in which the five atom ring is unsaturated at positions 2-3. Similarly, penems contain a sulfur atom at position 1 of the five atom ring, while |FRAME: Carbapenems carbapenems| contains a carbon atom in that position |CITS: [16413506]|. In |FRAME: Cephams cephams| and |FRAME: Cephems cephems| the &amp;beta;-lactam ring is fused to a six-atom ring (a 4:6 strcuture) that contains a sulfur atom at position 1. In cephams the ring is saturated, while cephems have an unsaturated bond at positions 3-4 |CITS: [1164170][12722308]|. 1-Oxocephams/1-oxacephems conain an oxygen atom at position 1 |CITS: [11671696][11078187]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="Beta-Lactams" />
